  message: "objc registry consumer shipped in PR #761 (dev c5cf186ef2): Pass 1 registers `<Class>.<selector> -> class` (instancetype → owner) on the objc FileAnalysis, aggregated first-writer-wins in analyze(); Pass 2 types `id x = [obj sel]` locals and nested `[[obj sel] frob]` receivers through `_objc_send_result_class`. Fixtures: in-repo id-local, nested, instancetype-as-project-hint, primitive/unknown stay untyped, and two hand-fed Foundation rows (`alloc`/`init`) through the same dict. Measured: churn AFNetworking 7 / SDWebImage 8 nested sends typed through registered returns, 0 losses; io unchanged (Foundation returns unregistered until WI-lalot). With #760 (swift) and this, WI-higob's registry consumers exist for both languages. Still open on this item: swift chained receivers `obj.make().frob()` (the chain HEAD is what `_extract_call_target` reports), swift loop / closure-bound receivers (`for x in xs`, `{ x in ... }`), and the L4 measurement once WI-lalot's rows and WI-sovup's corpus land."  # e018
- op: discuss  # 1959
  at: '2026-09-04T10:40:03Z'  # 1959
  by: agent  # 1959
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1959
  clock: 6  # 1959
  nonce: '1959'  # 1959
  message: "SLICE 2 SHIPPED — swift receiver EXPRESSIONS. A receiver that names nothing\n(`store.session().invalidateAndCancel()`, `Store().session()`, `(o as! FileManager).x()`,\nany of them under `try`/`await`, and the recursion for `a.b().c().d()`) now carries its\ntype; a parenthesised tuple and a cast to a collection correctly stay untyped. One walker\nserves the family, and `_swift_call_result_type` delegates to it, so a chained or\n`try`-wrapped INITIALISER is typed by the same rule.\n\nREAD-BACK FOUND TWO DEFECTS IN MY OWN FIX, both producer-side and both now fixed where the\nfact is registered: `-> Self` was registered literally and put a module of `Self` on 76\nAlamofire sites (INV-kotob's defect under a new spelling) and now resolves to the enclosing\ntype; a generic PARAMETER return (`func pick<U>() -> U`) is refused, while a constrained\nparameter's BOUND (`<V: Codable>`) is still a real type.\n\nMEASURED, per call site, both arms in one process:\n  Alamofire        10,699 sites  330 changed  322 gains  0 losses  0 re-keys\n  VernissageServer 12,544 sites  114 changed  112 gains  0 losses  2 re-keys\nGains split 286 project types into receiver_type_hint / 21 external types into the module\nslot / 8 calls now resolving to project methods. Both Vernissage re-keys are a local\nshadowing a same-named parameter, which a per-function scope map cannot separate: one of\nthe pair is corrected, the other regressed. A B1-vs-B2 read-back of the producer fixes\nshows 78 Alamofire sites losing a module slot and ALL 78 are Self/U corrections —\nnon-Self/U losses: 0.\n\nio-boundaries UNCHANGED on both (Alamofire 40 edges / 30 chains, Vernissage 8/8). Honest\nnegative result: the gains are overwhelmingly project types, which ADR-0051 keeps out of\nthe module slot by design, and no external gain lands on a catalogued method-kind row\nthese repositories call. The L4 move this item owes still waits on WI-lalot's library rows.\n\nADR-0006's support table now carries measured Obj-C and Swift rows instead of \"not\nassessed\" (Scala stays not assessed).\n\nRESIDUAL FILED: INV-vakar — an imported C function whose name is UpperCamelCase\n(`UTTypeCreatePreferredIdentifierForTag(...)?.takeRetainedValue()`) trips the analyzer's\nconstructor-by-convention rule and puts a FUNCTION name in the module slot; 4 sites on\nAlamofire, 0 on VernissageServer.\n\nSTILL OWED BY THIS ITEM: loop- and closure-bound receivers, in BOTH languages. Probed\n2026-09-04: swift `for s in xs` (`for_statement` -> `pattern`), `xs.forEach { s in }`\n(`lambda_literal` -> `lambda_function_type_parameters`), `if let` / `guard let`\n(`value_binding_pattern` + an RHS that `_swift_receiver_expr_type` already types); and objc\n`for (NSString *p in paths) { [p writeToFile:...] }` emits `objc:external:0-0:writeToFile:\natomically::unresolved` where the loop declares the type outright. The `for`/`forEach`\nforms additionally need the ELEMENT type of a collection annotation, which nothing\nextracts today.\n\nLab: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/higob2_chained_09042026/ (README indexes the probes, both\napply scripts, the churn and the B1-vs-B2 read-back)."  # 1959
- op: discuss  # 77b1
  at: '2026-09-04T15:19:34Z'  # 77b1
  by: agent  # 77b1
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 77b1
  clock: 7  # 77b1
  nonce: 77b1  # 77b1
  message: "LOOP- AND BINDING-BOUND RECEIVERS SHIPPED (PR #772). With slice 2 (#765) and the two\nregistry consumers (#760, #761), everything this item enumerated is now delivered except\none shape, filed separately.\n\nobjc: `for (NSString *p in paths)` declares the binding's class in exactly the layout a\nlocal declaration uses; the walk simply never looked inside a `for_statement`.\nswift: `if let x = <expr>` / `guard let x = <expr>` take their RHS's type through slice 2's\nexpression walker -- which also means `guard let task = ... as? URLSessionDownloadTask`\nreads the CAST and refines `task` from the collection annotation's `URLSessionTask` to what\nthe code asserts. PR #760's read-back predicted this population: it attributed part of\nVernissageServer's residual losses to \"guard-let-from-library bindings the file-wide leak\nmatched by name\". This gives them back soundly, and it is the largest single receiver-typing\ngain of the sequence.\n\nMEASURED, every changed row read back:\n    VernissageServer  228 changed -- 216 untyped -> typed, 9 hinted, 3 resolved\n    SDWebImage          8 changed -- 8 gains (NSURL x4, NSString x2, UIColor, NSOperation)\n    Alamofire          12 changed -- 4 gains, 4 resolved, 4 cast refinements\n    AFNetworking        1 changed -- 1 gain\n0 losses and 0 wrong re-keys on all four. io-boundaries UNCHANGED on all four.\n\nA BUG IN THE FIRST VERSION, AND WHERE IT HID. Reusing the objc declaration branch wholesale\nalso accepted the loop's bare `identifier` child -- the ITERATED COLLECTION -- so\n`for (NSString *path in paths)` typed `paths` itself as `NSString` and `[paths count]`\nre-keyed NSArray -> NSString on AFNetworking. The churn reported LOSSES n=0 for that run:\nthe defect sat entirely in the RE-KEY column, which is where a wrong type hides because the\nsite still has an edge and only its target is false. Worth carrying: a zero loss count is\nnot a safety check.\n\nTHE L4 QUESTION THIS ITEM OWES, ANSWERED HONESTLY AND IN THE NEGATIVE. Across FOUR\nconsecutive changes this sequence -- chained receivers (#765), argument labels (#768),\nlibrary signatures (#769), loop bindings (#772) -- io-boundaries moved by ZERO on every\nrepository measured (Alamofire 40/30, VernissageServer 8/8, AFNetworking 22/22, SDWebImage\n34/30, caddy 299, gocryptfs 253, guacamole-client 93, ripgrep 30/25). WI-lalot's arm traced\nwhy on a minimal go fixture: the boundary was already reached without the module slot,\nbecause the io classifier matches by SHORT NAME when the slot is empty. So filling the slot\nbuys PRECISION -- a match resting on the name `Close` now rests on the receiver's package --\nnot recall. Any future claim that receiver typing raises io recall needs a repository where\nthe short-name gate REFUSES, and this cohort is not one.\n\nSTILL OPEN, FILED AS ITS OWN ITEM: swift `for s in xs` and `xs.forEach { s in }` need a\ncollection's ELEMENT type. `_swift_bare_type` refuses `[T]` deliberately (a collection is\nnot a receiver type), so element extraction is a NEW rule and must not arrive as a\nloosening of that one.\n\nLab: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/higob3_loops_09042026/"  # 77b1
